<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanuki Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanuki Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, corresponding to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a>. It was the birthplace of the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a> and home of the Konpira shrine. Briefly conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chōsokabe Motochika</a> before Hideyoshi's invasion of 1585, it was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikoma-chikamasa">Ikoma Chikamasa</a>, who built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a> castle from 1588; under the Tokugawa its main domain was Takamatsu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanuki-province.jpg" alt="Sanuki Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Sanuki.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In one of the battles of the Genpei War, fought in <b>Sanuki Province</b> on Shikoku, near present-day Takamatsu, the forces of the Taira clan were defeated by the Minamoto.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He overran Awa, <b>Sanuki</b> and Iyo and by 1585 controlled most of Shikoku.</blockquote><a href="/state/chosokabe-clan-domain">Chosokabe clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Ikoma Chikamasa received <b>Sanuki</b> from Toyotomi Hideyoshi in 1587 and in 1588 began Takamatsu castle, a sea castle with moats fed by seawater.</blockquote><a href="/state/takamatsu-domain">Takamatsu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
